WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.016
Lengths, offsets, and counts are some of the most important fields in binary validation because they influence memory access.

2
00:00:08.016 --> 00:00:16.096
They tell the parser where data begins, how much data exists, how many records to expect, or how large an allocation might be.

3
00:00:16.096 --> 00:00:23.278
Because they come from input, they are not facts until the parser proves they make sense for the available data.

4
00:00:23.278 --> 00:00:27.061
A common mistake is to check only the beginning of a range.

5
00:00:27.061 --> 00:00:33.217
An offset may point inside the buffer, but the data that begins there may extend beyond the end.

6
00:00:33.217 --> 00:00:40.976
Defensive code checks both the start and the end of the range before reading, copying, allocating, or advancing a cursor.

7
00:00:40.976 --> 00:00:50.916
In practice, that means validating that the offset is within bounds, the length is within policy, and the expression that combines them cannot wrap around.

8
00:00:50.916 --> 00:00:53.417
Nested data requires nested validation.

9
00:00:53.417 --> 00:01:01.112
A top-level table may have a valid range, but each record inside that table may contain its own length or offset fields.

10
00:01:01.112 --> 00:01:07.845
Those inner values need to be checked against the region they belong to, not only against the whole file.

11
00:01:07.845 --> 00:01:13.167
This keeps one valid outer structure from becoming a pass for invalid inner access.

12
00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:17.656
Range checks should happen before pointer arithmetic or memory access.

13
00:01:17.656 --> 00:01:25.223
In C, computing an invalid pointer may already move the code outside the assumptions the compiler and platform expect.

14
00:01:25.223 --> 00:01:33.495
Using indexes and explicit integer checks before deriving pointers is easier to reason about and less fragile under optimization.

15
00:01:33.495 --> 00:01:38.112
Declared sizes should also be cross-checked against actual availability.

16
00:01:38.112 --> 00:01:44.012
If a header claims there are ten records, the parser should verify that ten records can fit.

17
00:01:44.012 --> 00:01:51.643
If an offset points to a payload, the parser should verify that the payload exists and belongs to the expected section.

18
00:01:51.643 --> 00:01:58.504
Validation is strongest when every size-related claim is connected back to bytes that are actually present.
